Capillary Bounce

Ingredients: GLASS HARMONICA + GQOM + BASS FLUTE + PREPARED ELECTRIC BASS + CONTACT-MIC PERCUSSION + 2020s ERA

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during an acoustics test inside a decommissioned municipal bathhouse, where a glass harmonica player noticed that damp fingertips, spinning glass bowls, and tiled reflections produced a trembling high-register field that stayed audible even above the hollow, low-slung impact of gqom drums. A Durban producer built sparse club patterns around the glass resonance rather than under it, while a bass flutist and prepared-bass player occupied the dark middle register left between sub-bass and fragile upper harmonics.

Capillary Bounce is a pressure-and-surface genre: brittle, ringing glass above; dry hollow percussion and sub pressure below; breath, scraped string, and tile resonance in between. The glass harmonica is the tonal authority. Sustained bowl tones, finger-drag friction, faint pitch warble, and slow beating between neighboring glasses determine the harmony and section length. The music avoids treating the instrument as an ethereal novelty. Its unstable resonance is the thing every other instrument must route around.

Gqom provides the rhythmic chassis: sparse, asymmetrical kick placement, low tom pressure, hard claps, blunt percussion, and large voids where the sub appears to fall through the floor. Rather than maximal layering, Capillary Bounce keeps the pattern skeletal enough that the decay of one glass bowl can remain audible through an entire rhythmic cell.

Bass flute occupies the shadow register. It uses air-heavy low notes, key clicks, tongue stops, overblown fragments, and slow semitone bends that emerge from the club pulse without sounding orchestral. Prepared electric bass is played with foam, clips, muted strings, bridge scrapes, and occasional e-bow sustain, creating short wooden thuds and dry metal chatter alongside restrained sub-frequency reinforcement.

The defining contrast is between continuous capillary motion and broken impact. Glass tones seem to wick outward through the room while the drums arrive as isolated pressure events. Tracks often feel suspended between ritual chamber music and a nearly empty dance floor where every physical sound matters.

Genre DNA

* Tempo & Pulse: 118–126 BPM, usually 4/4 with gqom-derived asymmetry, delayed kick responses, low tom clusters, clipped claps, and occasional 3+2+3 accent groupings. The pulse is heavy but deliberately underfilled.
* Harmonic Palette: Dorian, Phrygian, suspended minor fields, open fifths, minor seconds, major seconds, and slowly beating dyads derived from adjacent glass bowls. Harmony changes rarely; timbral beating and bass movement create most of the motion.
* Production Signatures:
  1. Wet-Finger Gate: Finger-friction noise from the glass harmonica opens narrow high-frequency percussion or noise bands, so more physical contact produces denser rhythmic detail.
  2. Tile-Decay Duck: Long tiled-room reflections are sidechain-ducked only during the heaviest kicks, allowing the room to surge back into the gaps.
  3. Capillary Pairing: Neighboring glass bowls are tuned slightly apart to create slow acoustic beating that becomes the tempo-independent harmonic clock.
  4. Hollow-Sub Separation: Gqom sub pressure and prepared-bass fundamentals are carefully separated by register; the electric bass often occupies upper bass while the synth sub handles only isolated low events.
  5. Flute Condensation Cut: Bass-flute breath noise is expanded and filtered during breakdowns, then sharply narrowed when the kick returns.
* Dynamics: Intensity grows through pressure, resonance length, and rhythmic clustering rather than volume. Early sections may contain only one glass tone, breath, and one low drum. Peak sections add paired glass beating, prepared-bass chatter, bass-flute counterlines, and denser kick displacement. Breakdowns often remove the sub completely while leaving room decay and finger friction exposed.

Scene & Origin Lore

Capillary Bounce formed in a disused public bathhouse in Durban that had been temporarily opened for sound-art rehearsals before renovation. The building contained drained tiled pools, narrow changing corridors, old copper pipes, cracked ceramic benches, and unusually long high-frequency reflections that made quiet friction sounds travel farther than expected.

Glass harmonica player Mira van Wyk began testing the room because the tiled walls exaggerated the weak upper harmonics of her instrument. Producer Sibusiso Mthembu, working nearby on sparse gqom material, heard the spinning bowls from another chamber and noticed that the instrument remained perceptible even when low drum pressure entered the space. Instead of sampling the glass and turning it into pads, he kept it live and removed percussion until the two could coexist physically.

Bass flutist Ayanda Khumalo joined after discovering that air-heavy low notes could sit beneath the glass without competing with the sub. Prepared-bass player Tomas Vale added a fourth layer using a short-scale electric bass modified with foam strips, binder clips, rubber wedges, and an e-bow. His role was not conventional groove support; he produced dry impacts, string scrapes, and occasional sustained fundamentals that stitched acoustic resonance to electronic bass.

Their first sets were called waterline sessions. A chalk line was drawn around the drained pool at the height where water had once reached. Each performer occupied a different point along the line, and no one was allowed to stand in the center. The arrangement therefore developed as a perimeter music: sound passed around the room rather than being projected from a stage.

The scene adopted a visual language of wet tile, pale ceramic, mineral stains, brushed steel, old copper piping, translucent instrument cases, towels, chalk marks, and practical work lights. The unofficial rule is that no reverb may be added simply to make the music atmospheric; every large spatial effect must imitate or derive from a real reflective surface.

