Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Waterline Without Water
- Story & Context: During the first full rehearsal, Mira notices that one glass bowl resonates noticeably longer when she stands level with the old mineral stain running around the drained pool wall. Sibusiso treats the stain as a structural marker and builds a kick pattern in which every fourth low hit is removed, leaving enough time for the bowl decay to pass around the tiled room.

Ayanda plays one breath-heavy bass-flute note beneath the strongest glass partial, while Tomas uses a foam-muted bass string as a soft wooden knock rather than a pitched line. Halfway through, the sub disappears completely and the track is carried by tile reflection, glass beating, bass-flute key clicks, and one prepared-bass scrape.

The final section restores the low pulse without changing the glass part, making the room sound smaller even though no microphone position has moved.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty tiled municipal pool with a distinct mineral waterline around the wall, glass harmonica on a low steel table at matching height, wet fingerprints on glass rims, bass flute leaning near oxidized copper pipe, muted electric bass on a folded towel, realistic documentary lighting. Spoken fragments limited to “same height,” “leave the fourth empty,” “the line is still here,” and “no water needed.”

Track 2: Copper Pipe Holds the Wrong Note
- Story & Context: Tomas discovers that an old copper return pipe rings at a pitch slightly sharper than Mira’s nearest glass bowl when tapped gently with a rubber-covered clip. Rather than retune either source, the band preserves the discrepancy and builds the track around the slow beating created when both tones overlap.

Sibusiso reduces the groove to kick, low tom, one clap, and isolated sub pulses. Ayanda uses bass-flute bends to pass through the unstable interval without settling on either pitch. Tomas alternates between the pipe tap and short e-bow bass tones, creating a second layer of drifting intonation.

Near the end, the pipe sample is removed but the musicians continue phrasing as if it were still present. The final glass tone is left alone until its beating becomes too faint to distinguish from the room.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close documentary photograph of an oxidized copper pipe crossing pale bathhouse tile, rubber-covered metal clip resting against it, glass harmonica bowls in soft focus, condensation droplets, bass-flute keywork catching one work light. Spoken fragments limited to “wrong note,” “keep it sharp,” “do not tune the pipe,” and “wait for the beating.”

Track 3: Towels Fold After the Sub Stops
- Story & Context: During a late rehearsal, a caretaker begins folding the old institutional towels stacked beside the changing corridor while the group is still playing. The soft fabric movement creates almost no pitch but repeatedly interrupts the room’s high-frequency reflection. Sibusiso records the folds and uses them as silent-feeling gates that briefly dull the glass-harmonica tail.

Mira sustains two neighboring bowls while Ayanda plays low air tones without full pitch. Tomas uses bridge scrapes and muted thumb attacks, leaving conventional bass notes out entirely. The gqom pattern becomes increasingly sparse until only one sub hit remains every eight bars.

In the final minute, the sub stops completely. The towels continue folding while the glass tones beat slowly above the room, and the recording ends on the dry sound of fabric placed onto ceramic.
